Use cpp-httplib and nlohmann/json. One Orderbook in the process.
A mutex around every request that touches the book.

Endpoints, same paths the Go balancer calls:

  GET  /status
      200 even when the book is empty, so the gateway can poll readiness.
      JSON for this process's one book: bids and asks as price levels.
      bestBid and bestAsk are numbers or null.

  POST /reset
      Empty the book.
      {"message":"All orderbooks cleared","booksCleared":1}
      booksCleared is 0 if the book was already empty.

  POST /trade
      Form fields, as Server.cpp reads them:
      orderid, tradetype, side, price, quantity, book.
      tradetype is GTC or FAK. side is BUY or SELL.
      Call Orderbook::add. Respond 200 with
      {"message":"Order placed successfully"}.
      The reference does not return the trades. Match that.
      400 if a field is missing. 400 if side or type is unknown.

  POST /cancel
      Form fields orderid and book.
      Call cancel. 200 if it returns true, 404 if it returns false.

  POST /batch
      JSON {"orders":[{"orderid","book","tradetype","side","price","quantity"}, ...]}.
      Add them in order, under the one lock.
      Response:
        {"processedCount":N,"results":{
           "<book>":{
             "tradesExecuted":...,"volumeTraded":...,
             "remainingBids":...,"remainingAsks":...,
             "bestBidPrice": <number or null>,
             "bestAskPrice": <number or null>,
             "bidLevels":...,"askLevels":...
           }
        }}
      volumeTraded is the sum of trade quantities from add().
      An empty orders array is 400.
      Skip an order with a missing id. Count only orders actually added.

The symbol in "book" is stored only so the results key matches what
the gateway sent. This process still has one Orderbook.

No printing inside add. The server may log one line per request.

