Simulation does what Simulation.go does:

  1. Reject an empty stock list, an empty symbol, priceMin > priceMax,
     and quantityMin > quantityMax.
  2. Start one engine per symbol, in parallel. Poll GET /status until
     200 or the wait expires.
  3. A second request for a symbol that is already starting must wait
     on the same task, not start a second process.
  4. POST /reset on each engine.
  5. Build random GTC orders. The gateway assigns ids, starting at 1
     and increasing. Bids first, then asks, matching the Go loops.
  6. Start the clock. POST /batch to each engine in parallel. Stop
     the clock.
  7. Return executionTimeMs, totalOrdersProcessed, and results[].
     Each result has the BatchResult fields plus symbol.
     Missing prices stay null.

Trade and cancel accept the same JSON as the Go handlers and forward
form fields to the engine. They start the symbol's engine if needed.

Reset clears every running engine. Status fans out to every engine.
Health reports each engine's port, url, and whether /status succeeded.
Engines lists the symbol-to-port map.

On shutdown, kill every engine process.

CORS allows the Next.js origin, http://localhost:3000.

