================================================================================
How it works / Notes: 
================================================================================

Four Moving Parts

  The order book library is the matching notebook. add, cancel, and the price counts live here. It has no ports and no JSON. Nothing else is allowed to reach into its maps. Everyone else calls those functions.

  engine_server is one running copy of that notebook with an HTTP window. One process, one book, one port such as 6060. It turns /trade, /cancel, /batch, /reset, and /status into calls on the library and sends JSON back. It does not know about the website, and it does not decide which stock it is. The gateway makes that decision by which port it calls.

  The FastAPI gateway is the front door, on port 8000. It is the only address the website uses. It keeps a table of symbol to port. A simulation of AAPL and GOOG makes it start two engine_server processes, invent the random orders, give them ids, reset each book, and POST /batch to each port at the same time. Then it stitches the two JSON replies into one response. A single trade goes to the one engine for that symbol, starting it first if needed. On shutdown it kills those engine processes.

  The Next.js website is the form and the results table, on port 3000. It sends the simulation JSON to port 8000 and draws whatever comes back. It never learns an engine port.


Step by Step 

  -> You fill out a form in the website.

  -> The website sends that form to the gateway.

  -> The gateway starts one engine for each stock.

  -> Each engine listens on its own port and holds one order book.

  -> The gateway remembers which port belongs to which stock.

  -> The gateway creates the random orders and gives each order an id.

  -> The gateway sends AAPL's orders to AAPL's port, and GOOG's orders to GOOG's port.

  -> The engine receives those orders and calls the library.

  -> The library matches the orders and updates that engine's book.

  -> The engine turns the results into JSON and sends them back to the gateway.

  -> The gateway combines every stock's JSON into one reply.

  -> The website shows that reply.